From PHP CS Fixer to DressCode
******************************

.[perex]
The `@PSR12`, `@PER-CS` and `@Symfony` sets have matching standards in DressCode, and `dresscode migrate config` translates your whole `.php-cs-fixer.dist.php` into `dresscode.neon`. Instead of fixers and their options, you then write how the code should look: where PHP CS Fixer needs two fixers and six options for the backslash before global names, one section is enough here. And many risky rules are not risky here, because the syntax tree tells the safe case from the unsafe one.


DressCode as an Alternative to PHP CS Fixer
===========================================

In daily work, little changes: `check` takes the place of `fix --dry-run`, and `fix` stays `fix`. What you gain: the same `dresscode fix` can also upgrade the code to a newer PHP and to new versions of Nette, Symfony or Laravel.

After PHP CS Fixer formatted Laravel with its set for [PER Coding Style |https://www.php-fig.org/per/coding-style/], DressCode still found 1,201 places where the code does not follow the standard, and 7,497 in Symfony 3.4. Part of the difference comes from the versions: PHP CS Fixer implements PER Coding Style 3.0, DressCode 3.1. In the other direction, PHP CS Fixer changed 9 places in Laravel after DressCode; [the comparison |how-it-works#PER Coding Style and PSR-12 Compared to PHP CS Fixer and PHP_CodeSniffer] explains where they come from. Measured in October 2026 with PHP CS Fixer 3.95.

For a project that has to stay on PHP 7, PHP CS Fixer is the better choice: it runs on PHP 7.4. DressCode needs PHP 8.4 to run and checks code written for PHP 8.0 or newer.


Before You Start
================

The [common steps |migration] apply to every tool; this page covers only what is different with PHP CS Fixer.

The main thing first: **the configuration of PHP CS Fixer is a PHP file that has to be executed**, and it returns an object of its library. So the `dresscode migrate config` command runs it in a process of its own, with the autoloader of the project loaded, and whatever the file prints or does stays in that process. DressCode itself may be installed anywhere, but PHP CS Fixer must still be in the project: in `vendor/` as a dependency, as a phar from phive in `tools/php-cs-fixer`, as the package `php-cs-fixer/shim`, or installed by Composer on its own in `tools/` or `vendor-bin/`. The file runs only for the rules; DressCode reads the paths from its code without running it. When the classes of PHP CS Fixer are missing, `migrate config` stops with a configuration error and exit code `3`:

/--pre .[terminal]
Error: File `/var/www/shop/.php-cs-fixer.dist.php` needs the classes of PHP CS Fixer, which DressCode finds neither in `vendor/` nor in `tools/`: Class "PhpCsFixer\Finder" not found.
\--

So convert the configuration before you remove PHP CS Fixer from the project.


Converting the Configuration
============================

```shell
dresscode migrate config .php-cs-fixer.dist.php
```

Sets are translated to standards: `@PSR1`, `@PSR2` and `@PSR12` to `psr12`; `@PER`, `@PER-CS` and their numbered variants to `perCs`, which matches PER Coding Style 3.1; and `@Symfony` to `symfony`, which does not have most of the phpDoc rules yet. `migrate config` reports sets with no counterpart and tells you where to start; for `@PhpCsFixer` it is `symfony`. Sets with the `:risky` suffix have no counterpart, because here risky fixes are not turned on by a set, but by the project for individual decisions (see below).

Fixers are translated to decisions, together with their options where an option has a counterpart. A configuration with the `@PER-CS` set, the `no_unused_imports` fixer and `trailing_comma_in_multiline` with `elements` for arrays, arguments and parameters gives this `dresscode.neon`:

```neon
use: [perCs]
imports:
	unused: forbidden

multiline:
	trailingComma:
		array: required
		argument: required
		parameter: required
		matchArm: optional
		closureUse: optional
		import: optional
		list: optional

classes:
	instantiationWrapping: keep
```

`no_unused_imports` is [`imports.unused` |decisions/imports], which, like the fixer, also counts a name in phpDoc as a use. `trailing_comma_in_multiline` was split into the [`multiline.trailingComma` |decisions/multiline] keys, one for each place where a comma can go; what `elements` did not list got `optional`, which means the comma is left as the author wrote it.

`migrate config` added `classes.instantiationWrapping: keep` by itself: from PHP 8.4, the `perCs` standard writes `new Foo()->bar()` without parentheses around `new`, while the `@PER-CS` set leaves them as they are. A fixer with no counterpart is listed on standard error, and that gives you a list of things you need to decide about. Custom fixers from the `kubawerlos/php-cs-fixer-custom-fixers` package are translated too, as long as DressCode knows the construct they deal with. Which decision matches which fixer is listed in the table of [PHP CS Fixer rules |php-cs-fixer-rules].

What `migrate config` does not carry over, because in the Fixer configuration these are not rules:

- **The Finder, beyond its paths.** `migrate config` reads `in()`, `exclude()`, `notPath()`, `notName()` and `append()` with a literal or `__DIR__` from the code of the configuration and writes them to `paths` and `excludePaths` by itself. It lists `filter()`, `depth()` and the other conditions of the Finder among the warnings, and so it does with a call whose argument cannot be read without running it, such as `in($dirs)`; rewrite those by hand. The patterns are described in [Configuration |configuration#Paths].
- **Indentation and line endings** (`setIndent()`, `setLineEnding()`). The standard sets them. If you use none, add the decisions `indentation.unit` and `file.lineEnding`; without a standard, `migrate config` reminds you of them by itself.
- **`setRiskyAllowed(true)`**. DressCode does not give permission for risky fixes to the whole configuration, but to individual decisions: their paths go in the `fixRisky` list. Without that list, risky fixes are only reported.


One Thing, One Key
==================

In PHP CS Fixer, several rules share one construct, and the result comes from how they work together. A project using PER Coding Style that wants to write global classes with a backslash, `\Exception`, and functions without one, except those PHP can optimize, needs two fixers and six options, and one of the options means the opposite of what it seems to say: `import_classes => false` does not mean "do not import", but "rewrite imported classes to the fully qualified name". On top of that, it needs `no_unused_imports`, so that the imports left over after the rewrite disappear:

```php
$config->setRules([
	'@PER-CS' => true,
	'native_function_invocation' => [
		'include' => ['@compiler_optimized'],
		'scope' => 'namespaced',
		'strict' => true,
	],
	'global_namespace_import' => [
		'import_classes' => false,
		'import_constants' => false,
		'import_functions' => false,
	],
	'no_unused_imports' => true,
]);
```

In DressCode, these are keys of one section, [`qualification` |decisions/qualification], and each one names what it wants; `imports.unused` removes the unused imports:

```neon
use: perCs

qualification:
	class:
		global: backslashed
	function:
		global: bare
		optimized: backslashed

imports:
	unused: forbidden
```

From this code:

```php .[before]
<?php

namespace App\Model;

use DateTimeImmutable;
use InvalidArgumentException;

class Cart
{
    public function add(array $items, string $sku): DateTimeImmutable
    {
        if (strlen($sku) === 0 || in_array($sku, $items, true)) {
            throw new InvalidArgumentException('Invalid SKU');
        }
        return new DateTimeImmutable(\date('Y-m-d'));
    }
}
```

both tools make this:

```php .[after]
<?php

namespace App\Model;

class Cart
{
    public function add(array $items, string $sku): \DateTimeImmutable
    {
        if (\strlen($sku) === 0 || in_array($sku, $items, true)) {
            throw new \InvalidArgumentException('Invalid SKU');
        }
        return new \DateTimeImmutable(date('Y-m-d'));
    }
}
```

Except for one place: PHP CS Fixer qualifies `in_array($sku, $items, true)`, because `in_array` is in the `@compiler_optimized` list, while DressCode leaves it bare, because the compiler speeds up only a search in an array written directly in the code, not in a variable. DressCode decides by the call, not by the name. It removes the backslash from `date()` without risk when the configuration says `nameResolution: certain`, that is, that no namespace of the project declares a function `date()`; otherwise it is a risky fix, just as `native_function_invocation` is a risky fixer. The rest of the section, constants and imports of global names, is described in [How Global Names Are Written |php-functions-in-namespaces#How Global Names Are Written].


Risky Rules
===========

PHP CS Fixer marks a rule as risky when, on an array of tokens, it cannot tell a safe case from a dangerous one, and it leaves the decision to you. A typical example is `ternary_to_elvis_operator`: it cannot tell whether the condition of a ternary operator is a variable or a function call with a side effect, so it watches only for `++` and `--`, and the rest is up to you.

DressCode does not have this split, because the tree answers the question: the decision [`expressions.ternary.short` |decisions/expressions] shortens a ternary operator only when the condition is safe to evaluate twice, and it leaves a function call alone. The same goes for other rules that were risky for the same reason.

Fixes that can change the behavior of the program are a different thing. DressCode does not turn them on with a special set. It reports them as risky, place by place, and for each one it says what would decide it: `mistakes.strictArgument` adds the parameter `$strict = true` to `in_array()` right away where the [types |phpstan-types] from PHPStan say an integer is searched for among integers, and elsewhere it only reports the fix as risky. A risky fix is made only when you list its decision in the `fixRisky` list in the configuration, or for one run with `--fix-risky`; until then it is a warning where types would decide it, and an error where it changes behavior by its nature, like adding `declare(strict_types=1)`. Writing a decision that changes the meaning of code, like `expressions.comparison.loose: forbidden`, does not yet mean you agree with its fixes; you agree only by listing it in `fixRisky`.

Some fixers form a special group, such as `is_null`, `no_alias_functions` and `dir_constant`: PHP CS Fixer marks them as risky because someone could declare a function of the same name in a namespace. In DressCode, the configuration settles this doubt. With the `nameResolution: certain` key and the lists in the `namespaces` key, DressCode knows what is in your namespaces, and the corresponding fixes are safe; see [Functions and Constants in Namespaces |php-functions-in-namespaces].


Commands
========

| PHP CS Fixer | DressCode |
|---|---|
| `php-cs-fixer fix --dry-run` | `dresscode check` |
| `php-cs-fixer fix --dry-run --diff` | `dresscode check --diff` |
| `php-cs-fixer fix` | `dresscode fix` |
| `php-cs-fixer fix --config=file` | `dresscode fix --config file` |
| `php-cs-fixer fix --rules=name` | `dresscode fix --set path=value`, which adds the decision to the configuration, or `dresscode fix --only path`, which runs only the ones you name |
| `php-cs-fixer fix src --rules=@PSR12` | `dresscode fix src --use psr12` in a project without a configuration |
| `php-cs-fixer fix --format=checkstyle` | `dresscode check -f checkstyle` |
| `php-cs-fixer fix --allow-risky=yes` | `dresscode fix --fix-risky`; permanently, a list of decisions in the `fixRisky` key |
| `php-cs-fixer describe name` | `dresscode explain path` |
| `.php-cs-fixer.cache` | the cache is on by itself; `--no-cache` bypasses it |

The exit code of PHP CS Fixer is a bit mask (8 means violations found, 16 a configuration error, and so on), while DressCode has one scale for every command, see [Exit Codes |cli#Exit Codes]. A CI script that evaluated the mask needs one change.


What PHP CS Fixer Did Not Have
==============================

- **PER Coding Style 3.1.** PHP CS Fixer implements version 3.0 (the `@PER-CS3x0` set), while the `perCs` standard implements version 3.1. The places DressCode still finds after PHP CS Fixer (the numbers are at the top of this page) are places where PER Coding Style wants one item per line, a heredoc indented with the surrounding code, or a comma after a multiline list, see [the comparison |how-it-works#PER Coding Style and PSR-12 Compared to PHP CS Fixer and PHP_CodeSniffer].
- **A message at every place, including what the fix can change.** For a file, `php-cs-fixer fix --dry-run -v` prints the names of the fixers that would change it, and with `--diff` the change itself; the `checkstyle` format has one entry per fixer and file, with no line number. `php-cs-fixer describe` explains why a fixer is risky, once for all places; for `strict_param`, it says `Risky when the fixed function is overridden or if the code relies on non-strict usage.` With `--allow-risky=yes` it then adds `true` to every `in_array()`, without it to none. DressCode judges each call separately and says why a risky one is risky, as [Risky Rules |#Risky Rules] shows. The same output also shows an overriding method whose parameter is named differently than in the parent, and a call to a method the library marked as deprecated; PHP CS Fixer reports neither of them, not even with the `@PhpCsFixer:risky` and `@Symfony:risky` sets. The full output is shown on the [home page |@home#More Than How the Code Looks].
- **Suppressing on a line.** PHP CS Fixer cannot turn off a rule for one line or for a block, only for a whole file through the Finder. Here you have `// dresscode:ignore path` for that, and the pair `dresscode:disable` and `dresscode:enable`, see [Suppressions and the Baseline |suppressions-and-baseline].
- **Different values for part of the project** are written as an override in the `overrides` key, with no second configuration file; a decision there gets a different value, or `keep`, see [Configuration |configuration#Different Values for Part of the Project].
- **Trying a decision without losing your configuration.** In PHP CS Fixer, `--rules` replaces the rules from the configuration file, so a fixer you try runs without the rest of your set and with default options, unless you spell out others in JSON. In DressCode, `dresscode fix --set path=value` adds the decision to the rest of the configuration, so its changes come out already formatted by your standard, see [Configuration |configuration#One Decision for One Run: --set and --only].
- **Baseline.** A decision that your code does not meet yet, and that cannot be fixed for you, can be turned on right away: the `dresscode baseline` command records today's violations, and only new ones will be reported. PHP CS Fixer does not have one; how DressCode combines it with fixes is described in [Baseline |suppressions-and-baseline#baseline].
- **Your own rule** is written against the tree, not against tokens. The [tutorial |custom-rule] takes one afternoon, and if you have a custom fixer, a [separate page |porting-rules] shows how to port it.

What is different for every tool is summed up in [the common steps |migration#What Will Be Different].

{{maintitle: PHP CS Fixer Alternative: Migrate to DressCode}}
{{description: A PHP CS Fixer alternative: @PSR12, @PER-CS and @Symfony have matching standards, one command converts .php-cs-fixer.dist.php, and you describe the code instead of fixers.}}
